#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073598 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6447abce-804f-5569-90ef-32d989cc7a06" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Similarity Ratio Between Two Similar Polygons"}] :question/id #uuid "da7af116-26c8-56e4-a6e5-b195cbb7699e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2968" :question/problem "Given that the two polygons shown below are similar, determine the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **larger** polygon to the **smaller** polygon.*\n\n![](images/f4/f4da94c11054e9e42b7dc9f6c085d3c6be5fdf026ba9d913fb7b060d56221967.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides. \n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP.$ Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD,$ which is  $\\overline{BC}.$ \n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073599 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63b3448f-fe75-5152-a3c0-00b5129375c5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Shapes Similar to a Given Polygon"}] :question/id #uuid "fd7f22e7-9aba-5936-beb1-56d3a5e016cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2969" :question/problem "Which of the following quadrilaterals are similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ (other than $\\mathcal R$ itself)? \n\n![](images/5a/5a7c9208a16a639ef6b08855a6caaacb39c121dd4ccf45271d98fcc70f1a8fed.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\mathcal{R}$ is a rectangle, meaning all the angles measure $90^\\circ,$ and it has $2$ pairs of parallel congruent sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ The polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rectangle, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{3}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ Indeed, $\\mathcal{P}$ is a rectangle. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{S}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ because $\\mathcal{S}$ is not a rectangle (it's a parallelogram).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073600 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77da466d-77c4-5ea7-bedb-753601111c40" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Similarity Statement Between Two Similar Polygons"}] :question/id #uuid "98596871-dc8e-5083-a773-1a70ffe1a159" :question/math-academy-id "e-2970" :question/problem "Given that the two quadrilaterals below are similar, write down a correct similarity statement.\n\n![](images/bf/bfff4195302736df01985ca9db83d373243833023003730004cd93512467e4f4.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is $360^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {55}^{∘}+m∠B+{90}^{∘}+{125}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+{270}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠K+m∠L+m∠M+m∠N & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {55}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}+m∠M+{125}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠M+{270}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠M & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠K,\\,∠D≅∠N,\\,∠C≅∠L≅∠B≅∠M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo far, we have that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $K,$ and\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $N.$\n\nFrom the angle measurements, it's unclear whether $C$ corresponds to $L$ or $M$ (these are all right angles). However, $C$ sits between a $125^\\circ$ angle and a $90^\\circ$ angle, and so does $M$ (whereas $L$ does not). So, we also have that \n\n- $C$ corresponds to $M,$ and\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $L.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct similarity statement is \n\n$$\nABCD \\sim  KLMN.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073601 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dca2a1be-4373-5572-9cf4-3c634079b1e9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Lengths of Sides for a Polygon Given Dimensions of a Similar Polygon"}] :question/id #uuid "8fda1604-bea3-5061-9a3f-a0ec86f95a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-2972" :question/problem "Given that $ABCD \\sim A'B'C'D',$ $AB = 3\\,\\mathrm{ft},$ and $BC = 4\\,\\mathrm{ft},$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'?$\n\nI. ${A'B'} = 15\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 20\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n\nII. ${A'B'} = 30\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 44\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n\nIII. ${A'B'} = 18\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 24\\, \\mathrm{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- For option I, the ratios are the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{15}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I indeed gives possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\n- For option II, the ratios are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{30}=\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{44}=\\frac{1}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option II does not give possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\n- For option III, the ratios are the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{18}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{24}=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III indeed gives possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6447abce-804f-5569-90ef-32d989cc7a06" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Similarity Ratio Between Two Similar Polygons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f70ab3-d1fd-5534-ae2c-bd54b0d418dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516157} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf243e1-daf6-5dbe-8ce6-ed87a3099ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516203} {:answer/id #uuid "6caa123f-3987-50a3-bd49-4971d88b75b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516352} {:answer/id #uuid "a21206bd-ef41-5608-b022-b7d66d665a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516445} {:answer/id #uuid "d07eb44e-094c-5aa7-a98b-5379e72cbf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d07eb44e-094c-5aa7-a98b-5379e72cbf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516553} :answer-field/id #uuid "25001951-6cdd-5c9e-8efc-d2bd29e9bd9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c6e7fd6-6ef2-5487-99ae-05f731828948" :question/math-academy-id "q-121032" :question/problem "![](images/39/392309f189b8840862795d3b111c20956ec2366b349c6a60a6b0288052d451c2.png)\n\nGiven that $△ABC∼△VTU$ (picture is not to scale), find the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **smaller** polygon to the **larger** polygon.*" :question/worked-solution "The similarity ratio is the ratio of the corresponding sides.\n\nSince $△ABC∼△VTU,$ the side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ corresponds to the side $\\overset{―}{TU}$\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{TU} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63b3448f-fe75-5152-a3c0-00b5129375c5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Shapes Similar to a Given Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc0b8b2-179d-59ee-be74-60876929c3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516144} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecc9ee7-1c24-578d-8c59-028a37ce7c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516236} {:answer/id #uuid "68e30e74-d0a8-5bc3-8434-a609f8e1e5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516339} {:answer/id #uuid "7410a378-e6fc-5722-9555-785db18e4f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516370} {:answer/id #uuid "76601693-bc59-54d3-acb6-5642e2b10ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R,$ $S,$ and $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68e30e74-d0a8-5bc3-8434-a609f8e1e5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516339} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2b35f2c-efc3-59cb-b3ac-4a86eb509d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8747f1cc-7c01-5370-9ad7-927463f6175f" :question/math-academy-id "q-121036" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4a79a8b0eb2c0c79b1cdcb0623dc913169db8a46aab9b0a18c23f96ac61a0cd.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $P$ (other than $P$ itself)?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $P$ is a kite with a pair of opposite congruent angles and $2$ pairs of congruent sides. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $R$ is *not* similar to $P$ because $R$ is not a kite.\n\n- $S$ is similar to $P.$ Indeed, $S$ is a kite. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2.5}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\n- $T$ is similar to $P.$ Indeed, $T$ is a kite. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2.5}{2.5}=\\frac{5}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ and $T$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dca2a1be-4373-5572-9cf4-3c634079b1e9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Lengths of Sides for a Polygon Given Dimensions of a Similar Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e4d84a-1733-5b6a-8de9-415d54c135e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516188} {:answer/id #uuid "8d115afb-90af-52ac-9dba-3f7e51b99e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516408} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc8dd87-daad-5359-95f9-c362d3a2bcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516434} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b4bc73-4915-5a93-8f69-3c7b84ab18ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516563} {:answer/id #uuid "de2f5f31-5294-57d7-b388-91a3696fbb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d115afb-90af-52ac-9dba-3f7e51b99e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516408} :answer-field/id #uuid "535f178f-1bc4-52ea-8f27-866fded622bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11d089d5-e1bc-5fc5-afc2-069d5d90d5e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-121053" :question/problem "Given that $ABCD∼LMNO,$ $CD=4\\,m$ and $AD=1\\,m,$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $NO$ and $LO?$\n\nI. $NO=12\\,m,\\,LO=3\\,m$\n\nII. $NO=16\\,m,\\,LO=5\\,m$\n\nIII. $NO=10\\,m,\\,LO=2\\,m$" :question/worked-solution "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- Let's check for this in option I:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I gives possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\n- For option II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does *not* give possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\n- For option III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does *not* give possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77da466d-77c4-5ea7-bedb-753601111c40" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Similarity Statement Between Two Similar Polygons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "265bb299-b7de-584b-aa4d-8a6fb58c6501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼RSPQ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516186} {:answer/id #uuid "3f09aaa3-9879-538a-af7e-51e054b41b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼PSRQ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516237} {:answer/id #uuid "44d05074-7ea7-5102-8bc6-2cdae02f2abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼PQRS" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516251} {:answer/id #uuid "74a301d6-1665-5c48-9799-21482fc5cf59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼RQPS" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516372} {:answer/id #uuid "955defbe-ed2d-5d17-9905-b1f52f78332b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼SRQP" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f09aaa3-9879-538a-af7e-51e054b41b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼PSRQ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516237} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a75ccf-e4a8-5bd8-baa7-adb30b3b66e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7e53512-d30f-5e5f-8560-8aa96c633833" :question/math-academy-id "q-121176" :question/problem "![](images/14/145db132983ccf795c8fc466cd6e1ce77b7a33a3119fb281c3592ad5234cb08a.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" :question/worked-solution "Since, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠P,\\,∠B≅∠S,\\,∠C≅∠R,\\,∠D≅∠Q,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $P,$\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $S,$\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $R,$\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $Q.$\n\nTherefore, $ABCD∼PSRQ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77da466d-77c4-5ea7-bedb-753601111c40" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Similarity Statement Between Two Similar Polygons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038ab6e6-010a-58ad-b015-3510a7068a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼NKLM" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516120} {:answer/id #uuid "06963455-0bb5-572c-9fdf-d38c673c43f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼MNKL" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516125} {:answer/id #uuid "43b433bb-0495-5ef8-a9c8-bd3769eb7b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼NMLK" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516250} {:answer/id #uuid "ca01af76-b506-5ac2-b2ff-bc9d7b8a929e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼MLKN" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516539} {:answer/id #uuid "dee85f43-b139-5afb-a292-046a5c998f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼LKNM" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06963455-0bb5-572c-9fdf-d38c673c43f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ABCD∼MNKL" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516125} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2d05394-8b11-58dd-b0a5-7d79939aa2f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f26dd26e-832a-57e0-87b3-fbf105b066fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-53576" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9fa701b3efb9251e5591c40fe8c5011a73cd2b7794f6ce356860b2eeb1d8001.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is ${360}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {68}^{∘}+m∠B+{85}^{∘}+{111}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+{264}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={96}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M+m∠N+m∠K+m∠L & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {68}^{∘}+{96}^{∘}+m∠K+{111}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠K+{275}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠K & ={85}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠M,\\,∠B≅∠N,\\,∠C≅∠K,\\,∠D≅∠L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $M,$\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $N,$\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $K,$\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $L.$\n\nTherefore, $ABCD∼MNKL.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6447abce-804f-5569-90ef-32d989cc7a06" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Similarity Ratio Between Two Similar Polygons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be58a25-a177-5a2e-b607-0fc07b39bf72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516164} {:answer/id #uuid "26f8df91-a004-5ed4-a842-1ee3477c18d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516190} {:answer/id #uuid "501fee38-c464-5aac-b956-c55ca47fbb99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516278} {:answer/id #uuid "81fe1089-50b4-5826-addb-12509edadade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516387} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3ec2c0-e6c3-5717-9355-9c66c24d12d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26f8df91-a004-5ed4-a842-1ee3477c18d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516190} :answer-field/id #uuid "825ac6b4-8e09-54a8-93d8-364062e99d83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3223d2f4-ea53-55be-b26b-e4775a16b327" :question/math-academy-id "q-87955" :question/problem "![](images/83/83881bdbd7df1527338db3de5e88b69003b7d30c8c25d4583127b1579a28cf83.png)\n\nGiven that $△DEF∼△PQR$ (picture is not to scale), find the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **larger** polygon to the **smaller** polygon.*" :question/worked-solution "The similarity ratio is the ratio of the corresponding sides.\n\nSince $△DEF∼△PQR,$ the side $\\overset{―}{DE}$ corresponds to the side $\\overset{―}{PQ}$\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{PQ}{DE} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63b3448f-fe75-5152-a3c0-00b5129375c5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Shapes Similar to a Given Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fbcd74-c10f-55e8-ac05-f01fea77a1e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516147} {:answer/id #uuid "465109ee-90d8-50db-9713-4cfb3cbb5d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516256} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb24f3c-6f2d-5ebe-a9bf-1a71a8cb0f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516277} {:answer/id #uuid "54ee511f-911a-559f-8bfb-87b06f994f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516291} {:answer/id #uuid "bdee3bc0-bc14-5eac-af24-cf0cdd490fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54ee511f-911a-559f-8bfb-87b06f994f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516291} :answer-field/id #uuid "d26a1b39-5d9a-522e-b93e-64f3b7de831a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c8cab05-4b0d-578a-9cd0-38ffa882719a" :question/math-academy-id "q-87971" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1ad27414fe6da6009a54815aff3137365067218e5b73f62e34ef6d3ae2527058.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $T$ (other than $T$ itself)?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $T$ is a trapezoid with one pair of parallel opposite sides. Since the angles adjacent to the bases are congruent, it is an isosceles trapezoid. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $S$ is *not* similar to $T.$ The polygon $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{16}\\ne \\frac{4}{4}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* similar to $T$ because $Q$ is a right trapezoid since it has right angles.\n\n- $P$ is similar to $T.$ Indeed, $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{16}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77da466d-77c4-5ea7-bedb-753601111c40" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Similarity Statement Between Two Similar Polygons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "134b5911-296f-5829-9e61-d54ff2ee3c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "HJL∼VWY" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516149} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd819f4-acd1-5080-bf8c-438dd9f0eef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "HJL∼YVW" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516207} {:answer/id #uuid "359c7da6-462f-5ab6-b282-f7e995d4304b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "HJL∼WYV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516226} {:answer/id #uuid "5a739a57-f8a7-57f6-9f07-9369498af250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "HJL∼VYW" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516305} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a62020-643f-54eb-a449-fe33ee4964ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "HJL∼YWV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "134b5911-296f-5829-9e61-d54ff2ee3c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "HJL∼VWY" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516149} :answer-field/id #uuid "f127219a-5ce9-57cb-92d2-db82d4cf9049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20014b11-37e4-5374-8b1b-750de6a641aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-88008" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e7c6cb8f0d7746c1d923ed80a9e05ba4f8eeb9ea3ae88c7b08bcd02037d8ec8a.png)\n\nThe two triangles shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠H+m∠J+m∠L & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H+{69}^{∘}+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H+{109}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H & ={71}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠V+m∠W+m∠Y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {71}^{∘}+m∠W+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠W+{111}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠W & ={69}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the triangles are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠H≅∠V,\\,∠J≅∠W,\\,∠L≅∠Y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $H$ corresponds to $V,$\n\n- $J$ corresponds to $W,$\n\n- $L$ corresponds to $Y.$\n\nTherefore, $HJL∼VWY.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77da466d-77c4-5ea7-bedb-753601111c40" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Similarity Statement Between Two Similar Polygons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4823d242-c49d-56b4-aff3-48822453e2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RTUV∼NOQP" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516258} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbcb437-e4ac-5d10-a354-008c01636cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RTUV∼ONQP" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516307} {:answer/id #uuid "ae60cf2f-16ad-5289-8212-eb696f284bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RTUV∼ONPQ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516476} {:answer/id #uuid "b64145b0-6817-519c-8ee5-e35a44472707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RTUV∼QPON" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516494} {:answer/id #uuid "dce56259-996b-52e8-acfd-4e35a70c4345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RTUV∼NOPQ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bbcb437-e4ac-5d10-a354-008c01636cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "RTUV∼ONQP" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516307} :answer-field/id #uuid "74784b25-32a1-5400-b846-47a22b13397e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7546430f-d626-5e00-b8d1-dad9719eee9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-88010" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fca4f1d297c465ecf9da9bef652f1602edb6e6cd8887f6b3b8a83441a40871b.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is ${360}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠T+m∠U+m∠V & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R+{130}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}+{110}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R+{290}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠O+m∠N+m∠Q+m∠P & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {70}^{∘}+{130}^{∘}+m∠Q+{110}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠Q+{310}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠Q & ={50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠R≅∠O,\\,∠T≅∠N,\\,∠U≅∠Q,\\,∠V≅∠P.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $R$ corresponds to $O,$\n\n- $T$ corresponds to $N,$\n\n- $U$ corresponds to $Q,$\n\n- $V$ corresponds to $P.$\n\nTherefore, $RTUV∼ONQP.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dca2a1be-4373-5572-9cf4-3c634079b1e9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Lengths of Sides for a Polygon Given Dimensions of a Similar Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae7b35a-6093-5cbe-8d7c-e232bbd15615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516344} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd30f04-a917-5994-adcc-b8593c9f0de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516354} {:answer/id #uuid "6e38f0aa-f8eb-5da6-bbac-c3c3aa1785ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516356} {:answer/id #uuid "e9277e82-8ab0-53f4-b029-196ab30ea2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516595} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e74dec-c9c5-5c43-a3d2-8f466781a67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9277e82-8ab0-53f4-b029-196ab30ea2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516595} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dbb8f80-3824-574a-98ef-fd79e58caa94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d0a74cc-718a-5740-8268-b7272012d842" :question/math-academy-id "q-88034" :question/problem "Given that $△ABC∼△LMN,$ $BC=4\\,ft$ and $CA=2\\,ft,$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $MN$ and $NL?$\n\nI. $MN=2\\,ft,\\,NL=1\\,ft$\n\nII. $MN=12\\,ft,\\,NL=6\\,ft$\n\nIII. $MN=10\\,ft,\\,NL=8\\,ft$" :question/worked-solution "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- Let's check option I:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{1}=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I does give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\n- For option II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option II does give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\n- For option III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does not give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
